Opening Prompt
our healthchecks setup pages the on-call every time we do planned maintenance because cron jobs stop pinging while things are down. right now someone has to pause every check by hand before we start and remember to turn them back on after. need a way to schedule quiet periods in advance.

(50 words, states only the pain and the need, no design hints.)

High-Level Task Goal
Healthchecks monitors cron jobs by waiting for pings and alerting through channels when a check goes down, and pausing exists but is manual and resets the check. The outcome: a team schedules a maintenance window ahead of time, and affected checks stay quiet for the duration.

check still down at window end alerts once the window closes: yes

windows follow the check or project timezone: yes

pause/resume and existing API responses unchanged: yes

existing tests loosened or deleted: no

"wrap pause() in a cron job" accepted as a solution: no

done without the test suite passing: no

Decision Points
Better: quiet windows hook into the sendalerts worker path, where Flip rows are actually turned into pages, so alerts genuinely stop. Worse: the model just toggles status to paused, which resets state, or it only hides the check in the UI/API while the worker still pages everyone.

Better: when a window closes with the check still down, the pending down alert goes out at that point. Worse: flips swallowed during the window never fire, so a check can sit broken after maintenance with zero notification, which is worse than the original paging problem.

Better: window boundaries are evaluated in the check's own tz field, matching how its cron schedule is already interpreted. Worse: naive or UTC-only comparisons, so a 2 AM window for an America/Toronto job fires at the wrong time.

Difficulty & Codebase Understanding
Healthchecks is a self-hosted cron and heartbeat monitoring service in Python and Django, with a multi-version REST API, dozens of notification integrations, and a substantial Django test suite. The task spans the Check/Flip models, the API views and urls shared by v1 through v3, the sendalerts background worker, plus a migration and some UI. What makes it hard is the indirection: status changes become Flip records that a separate worker processes into alerts, so a window feature has to suppress at that seam rather than by mutating status, and it has to preserve the down alert still owed when the window ends. Per-check timezones add a second trap, since the cron schedule is evaluated in the check's tz and the window has to line up with that. A weak solution will look complete in the API and still page people, or silently lose alerts after the window.

